#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 -7x +13)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  +\\dfrac{3}4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-13 & =-({(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} {:answer/id #uuid "626efd7c-bc12-5b9b-928b-743c558ef6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245177} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99c3dc-ae1f-5598-a413-19b87a0bf35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245397} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc842e-e24a-567f-8bf4-a2a2a63772a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245580} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c838f7-516c-56c5-9e1d-df6702452695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} :answer-field/id #uuid "7819a661-bbd8-5b64-a989-336243949373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({k}^{2}-16k+8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-16k+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-56.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}-16k+8) & =-({(k-8)}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =-(k-8)^{2}+56 \\\\  & =56-(k-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} {:answer/id #uuid "9f60c504-aeae-5275-9938-cb7e27e9ec18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270685} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ada0ec-df6d-5400-b1fa-9d1ba5b69efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271041} {:answer/id #uuid "fb114b87-c749-5289-8848-51e528face0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271466} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a8a1e-0990-533c-8797-de1d653da457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} :answer-field/id #uuid "537b2e4e-ba82-5a3e-9315-02cb6492d8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e832f16e-81bf-5dd5-9dcf-748202928d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140318" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+4x-12)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b6c4f-e84d-5b59-96f6-ec5ff4ca3b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565761} {:answer/id #uuid "3662e5af-46a7-5722-9ccd-5d2228b374fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565764} {:answer/id #uuid "4b141f66-de8e-506c-a57a-d99a3a23fc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-76-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565794} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3c2400-2c72-5f8d-b4b6-ba5f48a46492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ff7a4b-bfcf-57eb-b578-4dd542e44b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb8a5938-134a-51c9-838d-3e4010a8ca50" :question/math-academy-id "q-140319" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-8y+60)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} {:answer/id #uuid "2def7528-a392-5c38-a317-849706e41d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565747} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8f5a47-f218-5790-b0cf-2c6bd714ec14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565949} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dc3995-7e49-5218-bd7f-84bc849ae212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565981} {:answer/id #uuid "f44e234d-afc4-503a-ae90-2371906eb7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e21de81-a1b0-5b51-8bf2-2764e594128c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c17160-e343-5b78-8eee-e55ba4967a40" :question/math-academy-id "q-140322" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-12x+35)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0710913b-7399-5b9e-a5a9-f4627344dd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565688} {:answer/id #uuid "0d6b0b59-5f4d-58eb-aec7-42d212af1618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565700} {:answer/id #uuid "6969fc79-b38b-551a-bfdf-07495efdaf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565842} {:answer/id #uuid "91d492cf-1c56-56df-bd04-4aabaf69aec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31-{(y+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565914} {:answer/id #uuid "fc78b4ea-4ddc-5ed1-8637-95f6e755fa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(y+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d6b0b59-5f4d-58eb-aec7-42d212af1618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565700} :answer-field/id #uuid "322303e8-364d-5afe-acc4-d76d39051ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f29e3f2-9a97-5ae1-80ba-f81c4f7c0fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140331" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-5y+6)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+6\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+6\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $6$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} {:answer/id #uuid "2c113b7e-7f2d-50c2-84be-19bb33799b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269664} {:answer/id #uuid "55abb69f-b764-531c-95eb-eeec736810ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270028} {:answer/id #uuid "d6232d29-c295-5652-a6f9-4789848d3505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271146} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5d1b87-4d59-56d8-ac27-ed5cb43e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} :answer-field/id #uuid "286cef5c-d3e8-5bea-859b-e4618279b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e4b3efa-abe1-5a91-86fa-e6dadf579120" :question/math-academy-id "q-140375" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}+10x-\\frac{11}{6}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{11}{36}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}) & =6({(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057bf8b9-f4be-51c0-a4d1-d57b29da8233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565683} {:answer/id #uuid "61f2ea1d-2d8b-55fa-8520-17155db3eebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{(x+2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565832} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfed3f5-06e5-5e82-b0a9-731fa897dd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566038} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f36cd8-e603-55a8-9ec8-ae98cefb08df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566051} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8880bf-ffee-5976-8095-806532a97526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8880bf-ffee-5976-8095-806532a97526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566098} :answer-field/id #uuid "8091fb4f-3520-5238-a1fe-8deaeb6d643a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67f49a8a-532a-55d5-814b-4932396dbbeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140406" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-x-3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+x+3)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-x-3 & =-({(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2a5228-4d34-5c57-8a6c-a3feb27ed440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565702} {:answer/id #uuid "2e001848-6114-55d4-898d-e807366debec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60-{(y-18)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565748} {:answer/id #uuid "4803ab05-0b65-5cb4-92e9-f03eef1b730f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565792} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4aefa9-79b2-5f3f-9f24-fa82ea18cebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{165}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565800} {:answer/id #uuid "564dbae7-bea0-5bb4-b219-b6f52f82ad54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a5228-4d34-5c57-8a6c-a3feb27ed440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565702} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ac210-0c5c-5b57-b96d-e01801ddf600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52455c8c-f427-542e-9cf2-e8b407fdf74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140407" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y-21$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b4d955-924d-5b66-994e-cd8d4afee070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+8)}^{2}+40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565722} {:answer/id #uuid "9fda3e7d-5801-5dc8-9c05-1ae2500e0fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565932} {:answer/id #uuid "afcdb75c-e813-595d-bae0-066b25d66f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565956} {:answer/id #uuid "d752feaf-59fd-5134-932a-9f884eace22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566026} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f4119d-6a4b-5ef7-897f-562be8a71f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+8)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afcdb75c-e813-595d-bae0-066b25d66f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565956} :answer-field/id #uuid "952c5bf3-9d6e-5030-8f7f-3c940131b3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fcb379b-2e62-54ea-ab65-5d0b75f0dbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140530" :question/problem "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+32x+40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} {:answer/id #uuid "67107a8a-2dd9-5dbc-b0a4-79d01366d76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270186} {:answer/id #uuid "e5de3296-93d1-57f6-a0c1-155f521a694c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271292} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9bacd1-e2fe-51ca-b317-8cb0d98826a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-4)}^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271365} {:answer/id #uuid "efc066ec-cde9-533c-9085-bb96bb129a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(5x+4)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} :answer-field/id #uuid "127abff6-4446-5351-8594-23776f79806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11a225fd-d96c-5a5e-9031-b51e03cdd249" :question/math-academy-id "q-140532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-40x-60$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f430bea-8bdc-5c1d-ad5c-0fb423bfc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{91}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270254} {:answer/id #uuid "7d098b28-9b36-5a5a-a306-848196bbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270364} {:answer/id #uuid "c20ec7e5-a2f5-52cc-b689-4b8b3c3e13ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270986} {:answer/id #uuid "f5575521-acc6-52d8-8ef0-33c9633f2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{109}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271424} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0d581-4e01-550f-8515-51c1277eb311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0d581-4e01-550f-8515-51c1277eb311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271443} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fb4a22-6bc6-599f-9a3e-03db139590ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "801928f5-f859-5bec-ad10-ef9e1c04ab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142071" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-11y+30)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076} :answer-field/id #uuid "321f92c3-450e-5fba-993b-2e46cd9808aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c39176-488f-543b-a2da-3abb77eaa53b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f40a4d7-d407-5619-8448-b04becc5c8a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96" :question/math-academy-id "q-218758" :question/problem "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311} :answer-field/id #uuid "02af0bbe-51a6-5d5a-8c08-cd4c322fac91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2b726d-3532-55e2-ab3b-c21e3a83a5cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316} :answer-field/id #uuid "61042d02-bc0e-5fb3-8749-0a4f5cca1d61" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b993c3c-e8bb-5de5-8511-a2d9d6d2b174" :question/math-academy-id "q-218759" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fa85ec-5f23-5583-a1e2-3b339c957d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565708} {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8b8549-d167-5182-a05e-094b9fd3a421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565853} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d26802-0604-5ec9-a6dd-63413f57143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565977} {:answer/id #uuid "dbffa828-6ec7-5c64-9988-48bed410abc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} :answer-field/id #uuid "109e1a5c-722e-5d5b-b384-b35a60da59b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e52c4055-fdc4-580f-9736-b9940edca056" :question/math-academy-id "q-25252" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{a}^{2}-10a+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b3902d-1015-53f0-995d-a66e100771ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(4x-1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565709} {:answer/id #uuid "307076da-9752-5562-860a-d357b7f3f900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(4x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565754} {:answer/id #uuid "3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565774} {:answer/id #uuid "86e1cc55-8aa1-5b03-a1af-07dc83b72500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565889} {:answer/id #uuid "b561b8c7-15f6-5cfb-b205-0138ec744c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565774} :answer-field/id #uuid "167b595a-7f5f-59ff-991b-3067a90e82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-52532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-4{x}^{2}+8x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a795cb-9f0e-5939-900e-adafa303f8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330103} {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} {:answer/id #uuid "72076787-7552-58b8-8ad4-00b516cbaf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "98ee28e9-1737-5e3b-81c5-05141104f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "f630c36b-13b8-5df3-93c0-7e5b92d1a0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d388fcd-2c84-5c86-86ee-03feb01e614c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a732d9-8b0e-58f3-ab88-f23eff634c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2204f3-0b9c-5716-8b12-1daaa8e9c98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565695} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcec967-e7ae-5ddf-bf71-07068fba0434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565797} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} {:answer/id #uuid "abd335e9-aa90-5300-ad70-e9bfcbdab375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565950} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a8f032-2fca-5906-b4f7-ab9bf75bf249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c32e90-669a-52ff-9868-695c63e890c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3f44aac-b56c-541f-849d-0822403501d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-52539" :question/problem "Rewrite $7{x}^{2}-21x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383a1a6d-35ea-5270-9184-75c2682d7396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565769} {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d2b6a-5266-5c53-ac87-e02a1827047e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565802} {:answer/id #uuid "c97303ff-3ba1-59f2-bae9-fcada6af4564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566004} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d06a-0da6-5642-842f-83c69a159e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fbd290-f3b1-5227-bd0d-a92509c57f4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71daf213-45ae-576f-97ae-9ffbff2f758a" :question/math-academy-id "q-52543" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ddff9c-ac54-5bdb-8e05-4c9e80b9afd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329975} {:answer/id #uuid "30d8498f-8b3e-524c-b327-f7f1af56ad01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330381} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ac45d-48a3-5d22-9f35-e862992b052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e12e2d-9ac1-56f3-b293-e94794a6a02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332078} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171} :answer-field/id #uuid "064cff71-f6ed-52e2-8855-063c335fc1b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b82526-21d1-5906-b903-379b7a33781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52544" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+6y+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
